23:03:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely get where you're coming from — a lot of people are taught exactly that simplified version in school: you ask, they have to give it. It's catchy, easy to remember, and captures the *spirit* of the law. But the reality is a bit more nuanced, and that nuance was there from the very beginning. What you’re describing is the **ideal**, but the actual law has always been a balancing act. Think of it like the First Amendment: we learn “freedom of speech,” but that doesn’t mean you can shout “fire” in a crowded theater, commit perjury, or publish someone else’s private medical records without consequence. We accept those limits because we recognize competing values (public safety, truth in court, privacy). FOIA works the same way. When President Lyndon Johnson signed FOIA in 1966, he explicitly said: *“A democracy works best when the people have all the information that the security of the Nation permits. No one should be able to pull curtains of secrecy around decisions which can be revealed without injury to the public interest.”* Notice the tension built right into that sentence: *all the information that security permits*, *revealed without injury*. The law’s own name — *Freedom* of Information — doesn’t mean *absolute* freedom; it means freedom *within* a framework that protects other important public and private interests. Your school lesson probably emphasized the “people have a right to know” part because that’s the revolutionary heart of the law: the default is disclosure, not secrecy. That’s a huge shift from how government worked before. But the exemptions (and the redactions that go with them) are the safety valves that allowed the law to pass and function in the first place. Without them, Congress would never have agreed to it, because genuinely dangerous or invasive releases would have been mandatory. So you’re right to feel that heavy redactions can undercut the promise. But the act of redacting is the government saying, “We’re giving you 95% of this document, as the law demands — we just have to black out the 5% that would reveal a spy’s identity, a sexual assault victim’s name, or a company’s proprietary formula.” That’s not a contradiction of FOIA; it’s the mechanism that tries to deliver the maximum possible information while respecting those other legal boundaries. It would be more accurate for schools to say: **FOIA means the government *must* hand over everything it can *legally* release, and it has to justify any part it withholds.** That’s less punchy, but it’s the truth.
