23:03:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're putting your finger right on the tension that has dogged this law since day one. I get why it feels like the name is either a promise—or a lie. But legally and practically, it's more accurate to think of it as a **contested space** than a simple either/or. What the law *actually* says is not "total freedom of information" in the absolute sense. The name is the title, not the content. The real meat is Section 552 of the U.S. Code, and it begins by stating a general right of access, then immediately lays out those nine specific, numbered exemptions. So from a legal standpoint, it's not a loophole; **the exemptions are part of the law itself**. The "freedom" is defined by the full text, not just the title. Why name it that, then? Because when it was passed in 1966, the radical shift was from a **presumption of secrecy** to a **presumption of openness**. Before FOIA, government agencies could withhold anything unless there was a specific law forcing disclosure. FOIA flipped it: now you can access *anything* unless it falls into one of those narrow, written exceptions. That was a revolutionary change. The word "Freedom" in the title captured that philosophical pivot, not a guarantee of absolutely unrestricted access. Think of other laws with broad names that don't promise the absolute: the "Patriot Act" doesn't mean everything in it is patriotic to everyone; the "Clean Water Act" doesn't mean you can drink straight from any river. Titles are bumper stickers, not contracts. Now, is it "false advertising" in the court of public opinion? I'd argue it's more of an **aspirational brand** that often over-promises and under-delivers when agencies abuse the exemptions. You're right that when an agency redacts entire pages with a broad brush, it can corrode the whole purpose and feel like a bait-and-switch. But the legal framework itself isn't a trick; it's an honest attempt (flawed in execution) to say, "Disclose everything except these specific, sensitive categories that we've named in advance." The name reflects the ideal; the exemptions define the reality. So, which is it? It's neither a pure "total freedom" mirage nor just a "nice name on loopholes." It's a **structural compromise** that, when working properly, forces the government to show you the goods while protecting genuinely dangerous-to-release information. The frustration you're voicing is exactly why FOIA battles keep ending up in court—because the line between "legitimate protection" and "loophole" is a constant fight, not a settled fact.
