23:05:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and you're not crazy for thinking that — a lot of people feel exactly the same way. When you see a page that's nothing but black bars, or when an agency takes two years to tell you "sorry, we can't confirm or deny the existence of records," it sure looks like the exemptions are just a permission slip to hide things. But here's the uncomfortable truth: the situation you're describing — a world where the government had to release *literally everything*, no exceptions — would actually make the FOIA *weaker*, not stronger. Here's why: **The Law Would Have Been Killed Before Birth** In 1966, Congress didn't pass FOIA unanimously as a feel-good measure. There was fierce opposition from agencies and from lawmakers who thought it would cripple government. The exemptions were the political price of getting the bill passed at all. Without them, there'd be no FOIA. You'd still be in the pre-1966 world where agencies could simply say "no" without any legal justification. So the exemptions aren't a later trick; they're the original compromise that created the right to ask in the first place. **Fear of Exposure Would Trump Transparency** Think about what "no wiggle room" would actually mean in practice: - The government would have to hand over the names of CIA sources in the field, getting people killed. - It would have to release the full medical records of veterans who filed disability claims, splashing their PTSD diagnosis or HIV status across the internet. - It would have to give out the proprietary formulas for new drugs under review, allowing foreign companies to steal billions in research. - It would have to disclose exactly where undercover FBI agents are operating and what their methods are, in real time. If that were the law, the government wouldn't just shrug and comply. It would do everything possible to avoid creating records in the first place — no emails, unwritten decisions, verbal everything. Or it would classify everything to the moon and make classification a de facto exemption. Real transparency would collapse because the exposure would be so reckless it would force a retreat into true secrecy. **The Name Was Always a Brand, Not a Contract** We keep circling back to the name. But calling it the "Freedom of Information Act" was a deliberate piece of advocacy — a banner to rally behind. It's like the "Affordable Care Act." The name expresses a goal: care should be affordable. It doesn't mean all care is now free. Similarly, FOIA's name expresses the goal: information should be free. But the full text of the law defines the boundaries. **The Real Problem Is Abuse, Not the Exemptions Themselves** You're absolutely right that agencies *do* use exemptions as excuses. They over-redact for embarrassment, not security. They hide incompetence under "deliberative process." They stretch "law enforcement sensitivity" to cover up misconduct. That's infuriating and it undercuts trust. But that's a problem of *enforcement* and *culture*, not an argument that the exemptions shouldn't exist at all. If you got rid of the exemption for personal privacy tomorrow, you wouldn't get more accountability — you'd get your neighbor's tax records and a massive backlash that would torpedo the entire law. Think of it this way: the tool is a scalpel, but some doctors use it like a machete. The answer isn't to ban scalpels. It's to demand that they use it properly, and to call them out when they don't. That's what the journalists in your documentary were actually doing — using FOIA's power, but also fighting the abuse of exemptions in court when necessary. They didn't get "everything, no exceptions." They got a release *despite* the government's attempts to hide behind those exceptions. The story works because the exceptions exist — and they were challenged. So I get the frustration. It looks like a scam when you see the gaps. But the alternative isn't a transparency paradise; it's a world where FOIA never passed, or where the response to a request is a simple "no" with no explanation at all, instead of at least a partial disclosure with a reason you can challenge. The name inspires; the structure allows it to function in the real world among competing dangers. The fight is making sure "exemptions" don't become a synonym for "whatever we feel like hiding." That's a fight worth having.
